```rust
use serde_json::Value;

pub fn solve(input: Value) -> Value {
    let mut jobs: Vec<(i64, i64)> = serde_json::from_str(&input).unwrap();
    jobs.sort_by(|a, b| a.0.cmp(&b.0));

    let mut finish_times: Vec<i64> = Vec::new();
    let mut previous_finish = 0;

    for job in jobs {
        let finish = previous_finish + job.1;
        finish_times.push(finish);
        previous_finish = finish;
    }

    serde_json::to_string(&finish_times).unwrap()
}
```

This function first parses the input JSON into a vector of pairs of numbers. It then sorts this vector by the first number in each pair, which is the arrival time.

Next, it creates an empty vector to store the finish times. It also initializes a variable to keep track of the previous finish time.

Then, for each job in the sorted vector, it calculates the finish time as the sum of the previous finish time and the duration of the job. It then adds this finish time to the vector of finish times. It also updates the previous finish time to be the finish time of the current job.

Finally, it converts the vector of finish times back into a JSON string and returns this string.

Please note that this function assumes that the input is a valid JSON array of pairs of numbers, where each pair consists of two numbers. If the input is not valid JSON, the function will panic.
